{"name":"Bungalow Taupe","hex":"#cebe9f","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bungalow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A single-storey house, typically with rooms all on one level, or sometimes also with upper rooms set into the roof space.","A thatched or tiled one-story house in India surrounded by a wide veranda; a similar house in this style."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bungalow"},{"term":"taupe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dark brownish-grey colour, the colour of moleskin."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark brownish-grey colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/taupe"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bungalow","blocks":[{"text":"A bungalow is a small house or cottage that is typically single- or one-and-a-half-storey. If a smaller upper storey exists, then it is frequently set in the roof and windows that come out from the roof. It may be surrounded by wide verandas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/149MyrtleReedsburgWI.JPG/330px-149MyrtleReedsburgWI.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bungalow","wikidata":"Q850107"},{"title":"Taupe","blocks":[{"text":"Taupe is a dark gray-brown color. The word derives from the French noun taupe meaning \"mole\". The name originally referred only to the average color of the French mole, but beginning in the 1940s, its usage expanded to encompass a wider range of shades."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taupe","wikidata":"Q2307593"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/StateLibQld_2_228966_Home_Building_Publishing_Company_home%2C_Design_No._3%2C_1939.jpg/500px-StateLibQld_2_228966_Home_Building_Publishing_Company_home%2C_Design_No._3%2C_1939.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thu